; UN-CONS backs the scavenger's pointer up to the free pointer it has just
; lowered (sys/ucadr/uc-storage-allocation.lisp, UN-CONS-1).  Load
; lisp/un-cons-gc-pointer.lisp first.  REGION-GC-POINTER is relative to the
; region's origin, as the free pointer is; MIT's microcode compared it with
; M-E, the object's end as an address, which is larger than any offset, so
; it never backed the pointer up, and the scavenger's pointer stayed above
; the free pointer, past words that the next object consed there would hold.
; The scavenger passing those words between the cons and the UN-CONS is
; planted here by setting the gc pointer above the free pointer first.
;
; The controls: the sum takes three words, its fourth given back, whatever
; the gc pointer; and with the gc pointer at the free pointer before it stays at
; or below it after.
(first (un-cons-sum)) => 3
(first (un-cons-sum 10.)) => 3
(un-cons-gc-pointer-ok) => T
; The gc pointer ten words above the free pointer before: backed up to the
; free pointer, an offset within the region, after.
(un-cons-gc-pointer-ok 10.) => T
(let ((r (un-cons-sum 10.))) (= (second r) (third r))) => T
